:: FirstTow {"title":"Chapter III / Sisters in Motion","scene":"yard"}
# Sisters in motion

@Chapter("CHAPTER III · MONTH TWO", "Control is difficult to invoice")

A man blocks the driveway of someone from the Wednesday circle. He is her ex. He
parks there to force a conversation.

You call it coercive control. Mars calls it a parking violation.

Control is difficult to invoice. Parking violations are not.

That night Mars tows his sedan. He pays $240 because the glove box contains
something he does not want the police to inventory. Mars places eighty dollars on
your kitchen table.

Within a week you have business cards for **SISTER SHIFT RECOVERY**. The logo is a
raised fist holding a tow hook. The slogan is worse.

::: dialogue {.dialogue speaker="Mars" mood="delighted"}
“Every time you turn theft into a workshop title, an angel loses her catalytic converter.”
:::

Which calls do you accept?

[[Only clear safety cases: blocked drives, abandoned cars -> EthicalRoute]]

[[Anyone your circle says has earned the inconvenience -> FavorRoute]]

[[Apartment contracts. Volume makes the numbers work -> ContractRoute]]

:: EthicalRoute {"title":"Chapter III / A Narrow Lane","scene":"yard"}
@do $heat += 1
@do $trust += 1
@do $debt += 120
@do $notes.push({id: $notes.length, text: "Sister Shift refused easy money and discovered ethics have overhead."})

@Chapter("BUSINESS MODEL A", "A narrow lane")

You take only the calls you can defend without changing the subject. There are not
enough of them. Fuel, permits, and the truck's mysterious thirst for transmission
fluid eat every dollar.

Mars never complains about the money. She starts checking job listings when she
thinks you are asleep.

[[Open the envelope on the kitchen table -> JulesArrives]]

:: FavorRoute {"title":"Chapter III / Community Standards","scene":"yard"}
@do $heat += 3
@do $leverage += 2
@do $debt += 40
@do $notes.push({id: $notes.length, text: "The definition of community safety expanded to include people Lenora disliked."})

@Chapter("BUSINESS MODEL B", "Community standards")

First you tow dangerous men. Then unreliable brothers. Then the mother of a woman
who mocked your breathing exercise online.

Mars says the boundary is getting blurry. You tell her justice is context-sensitive.

“Funny how the context keeps having your phone number,” she says.

[[Open the envelope on the kitchen table -> JulesArrives]]

:: ContractRoute {"title":"Chapter III / Scale","scene":"yard"}
@do $heat += 5
@do $debt -= 100
@do $leverage += 1
@do $notes.push({id: $notes.length, text: "Sister Shift signed its first apartment contract and stopped asking who called."})

@Chapter("BUSINESS MODEL C", "Scale")

The apartment manager pays on time. Tenants do not. You begin patrolling after
midnight, when expired permits are easy to see and exhausted people are slow to argue.

For the first time, Sister Shift has cash left after fuel.

For the first time, Mars stops telling you the names of the people whose cars you take.

[[Open the envelope on the kitchen table -> JulesArrives]]

